# honest-errors declared in .hd — the error-cadence module (honest-errors-architecture.md v0.2).
# A host error hook catches a raw failure (a browser JavaScript error object, or a server Python
# exception) and calls the `capture` door with that raw payload, the source it came from, the
# environment and timestamp the boundary already knows, a cadence the caller declares as data, and
# `now`. honest-errors normalizes the payload to one canonical ExceptionReport, enacts the declared
# cadence against its OWN persisted CadenceState, and returns one CaptureResult carrying the report,
# the caller's response, and the display actions due now. honest-errors never logs and never sends:
# honest-observe records the report from the result, honest-alerts delivers from the actions, and
# honest-errors depends on neither. It reads no clock and no environment — environment, timestamp
# and now are arguments (§1.5). It never raises; a failure comes back as a returned Fault (§6).

module honest-errors

  layer domain

  note "normalizes a raw browser or server failure into one canonical ExceptionReport and enacts the caller's declared cadence against its own persisted state, returning one CaptureResult of report, response and the display actions due now"

  # ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  # Step 1. The world, kept thin. honest-errors touches exactly one outside thing: honest-persist,
  # which holds its CadenceState between calls. The host that calls `capture` is represented by the
  # `entry` at the foot of the file, not by an input (a caller is not a resource the module reads).
  # The clock and the environment are NOT here: now, timestamp and environment arrive as arguments.
  # honest-observe and honest-alerts are CONSUMERS of the returned CaptureResult, not dependencies.
  # ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  inputs  = { "honest-persist" : "the module's own persisted CadenceState, read at the start of a capture to load the cadence state as it stood after the previous call (§5)" }
  outputs = { "honest-persist" : "the module's own CadenceState, written at the end of a capture so a held or scheduled display survives a restart; the orchestrator is the single writer (§5)" }

  # ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  # The foreign failure shapes. honest-errors does not own these: a browser decides what fields a
  # JavaScript error object carries, and CPython decides what fields an exception carries. They are
  # the world arriving, which is why `capture` is a real door and not a pure leaf. honest-errors
  # receives one of these per failure and reads it; it never constructs one.
  # ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  type JSErrorPayload = foreign "browser" note "one raw browser JavaScript error object, the value a host's window.onerror (or equivalent) hands honest-errors' capture door (§2). A browser owns the shape, so it is foreign: honest-errors reads the keys classify_js_payload needs — message, source, lineno, colno, stack, url, user_agent, timestamp and a context dict (§2) — and never defines them. A required key missing from it is what classify_js_payload returns a malformed_payload Fault for (§3)."

  type PythonExceptionPayload = foreign "cpython" note "one raw server Python exception payload, the value a host's HTTP-framework error handler or @catch_at_boundary hands the capture door (§2). CPython owns the shape, so it is foreign: honest-errors reads the keys classify_py_payload needs — exception_type, message, tb_file, tb_line, tb_function, traceback and a context dict (§2) — and never defines them. A required key missing from it is what classify_py_payload returns a malformed_payload Fault for (§3)."

  # ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  # The bounded vocabularies (§2.1, §4, §5). Closed sets of words, declared as sets wrapped in
  # vocabularies so a reader can list and count every allowed value and honest-test can enumerate
  # them. No free `str` stands where one of these belongs.
  # ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  set severities = {
    "debug"    : "diagnostic detail, the lowest grade",
    "info"     : "an informational event",
    "warning"  : "a recoverable concern",
    "error"    : "a failure; the grade every Python-path report carries and the default JS grade (§3)",
    "critical" : "the highest grade; a JS message whose text contains the token 'critical' (case-insensitive) earns it (§3)"
  } note "the five closed severity grades of §2.1 (SEVERITIES), ordered debug < info < warning < error < critical. The Python normalizer always stamps a report 'error'; the JS normalizer derives the grade with the js_severity predicate, which yields 'critical' only when the message text contains 'critical' matched case-insensitively and 'error' otherwise (§3). Declared as a frozenset, never a free string, so honest-test can enumerate it."
  vocabulary Severity = { severities } note "the closed severity grading an ExceptionReport.severity field carries (§2.1). Exactly the five SEVERITIES words and no other; js_severity returns one of them and both normalizers write one into every report."

  set environments = {
    "development" : "a developer's local or shared dev deployment",
    "production"  : "the live deployment serving real users",
    "test"        : "an automated-test deployment"
  } note "the three closed deployment environments of §2.1 (ENVIRONMENTS). The boundary that catches the failure already knows which one it is running in and passes it to capture as an argument; honest-errors never reads it from os.getenv or any other hidden source (§1.5, §3). Declared as a frozenset so honest-test can enumerate it."
  vocabulary Environment = { environments } note "the closed set of environment words an ExceptionReport.environment field carries and capture, normalize and both normalizers take as an argument (§2.1, §3). Exactly the three ENVIRONMENTS words."

  set response_modes = {
    "returned"     : "the caller gets the normalized ExceptionReport back as the response (§4)",
    "acknowledged" : "the caller gets a bare acknowledgement that the failure was captured, not the report (§4)"
  } note "the two closed response modes of §2.1 (RESPONSE_MODES). A ResponseSpec.mode is one of them, and it answers the second of the cadence's four questions: when a failure needs a response, does the caller get the report back or just a receipt (§1.2, §4)."
  vocabulary ResponseMode = { response_modes } note "the closed set of response-mode words a ResponseSpec.mode field carries (§2.1, §4). Exactly the two RESPONSE_MODES words; build_response reads it to decide what the caller's response is."

  set preset_names = {
    "silent"      : "respond Absent, display Absent: normalize and record only, no response and no display (§4.1)",
    "acknowledge" : "respond acknowledged, display Absent: return a bare receipt, no display (§4.1)",
    "report_back" : "respond returned, display Absent: return the report, no display (§4.1)",
    "notify"      : "respond acknowledged, display Once-Immediate: return a receipt and show the failure once, now (§4.1)"
  } note "the four closed preset names of §4.1 (PRESET_NAMES), the naming sugar a caller may use instead of writing a full Cadence. Each names a Cadence that falls within the actions §4 lists. The caller chooses the preset; honest-errors never picks one from the environment or any other hidden input (§4.1)."
  vocabulary PresetName = { preset_names } note "the closed set of preset names resolve_cadence and capture accept in place of a full Cadence (§4.1). Exactly the four PRESET_NAMES words; a name outside this set is what resolve_cadence returns an unknown_preset Fault for."

  set action_kinds = {
    "show" : "display this report now (a new immediate display, or a scheduled one whose time has arrived) (§5)",
    "hide" : "stop displaying this report (its held period has elapsed) (§5)",
    "send" : "deliver this report over a channel now (§5)"
  } note "the three closed display-action kinds of §5 (ActionKind). A DisplayAction.kind is one of them. The kind is a DECISION honest-errors makes about what is due; honest-alerts reads it and performs the delivery or the DOM change. Declared as a frozenset so the set of things honest-errors can ask for is closed and countable."
  vocabulary ActionKind = { action_kinds } note "the closed set of action-kind words a DisplayAction.kind field carries (§5). Exactly show, hide and send."

  set display_statuses = {
    "showing" : "the display is live and visible now (§5)",
    "pending" : "the display is scheduled and not yet due; it goes live when its `due` time arrives (§5)"
  } note "the two closed states a LiveDisplay can be in (§5, DisplayStatus bounded union): showing now, or scheduled and waiting. release_due flips a pending entry to showing when its due time has arrived; expire_elapsed removes a showing entry when its until time has passed (§5)."
  vocabulary DisplayStatus = { display_statuses } note "the closed set of status words a LiveDisplay.status field carries (§5). Exactly showing and pending."

  set duration_kinds = {
    "once"   : "the failure displays a single time (§4)",
    "period" : "the failure displays for a set period of seconds, carried in Duration.seconds (§4)"
  } note "the two arms of §4's Duration bounded union, expressed as a discriminant. A DisplaySpec.duration is 'once' with no seconds, or 'period' with a seconds count. A display declared once adds its DedupKey at most one live entry, so the same failure does not re-display while that entry is live (§5)."
  vocabulary DurationKind = { duration_kinds } note "the closed set of duration words a Duration.kind field carries (§4). Exactly once and period."

  set timing_kinds = {
    "immediate" : "the failure displays now (§4)",
    "scheduled" : "the failure displays at a later time, carried in Timing.at (§4)"
  } note "the two arms of §4's Timing bounded union, expressed as a discriminant. A DisplaySpec.timing is 'immediate' with no `at`, or 'scheduled' with an ISO-8601 `at` time. A scheduled display becomes a Pending LiveDisplay that release_due turns live when its due time arrives (§5)."
  vocabulary TimingKind = { timing_kinds } note "the closed set of timing words a Timing.kind field carries (§4). Exactly immediate and scheduled."

  set fault_codes = {
    "malformed_payload" : "a raw payload is missing a key its shape requires; returned by classify_js_payload or classify_py_payload (§3)",
    "unknown_source"    : "the source names no normalizer; returned by normalize (§6)",
    "unknown_preset"    : "a preset name the PRESETS table does not carry; returned by resolve_cadence, its message naming the presets it accepts (§4.1)"
  } note "the three closed fault codes honest-errors can return (§3, §4.1, §6). A Fault.code is one of them. Declared as a set so the catalogue of ways a capture can fail is closed and listable; the exact condition for each is stated in the function that returns it."
  vocabulary FaultCode = { fault_codes } note "the closed set of fault codes a Fault.code field carries. Exactly malformed_payload, unknown_source and unknown_preset."

  # ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  # The report IR and the fault (§2, §7). All records, no classes.
  # ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  type ExceptionReport = {
    exception_type: str
    message:        str
    severity:       Severity
    environment:    Environment
    file:           str
    line:           int
    function:       str
    traceback:      str
    context:        dict<str, any>
    timestamp:      str
  } note "the one canonical failure report both normalizers produce, so every downstream consumer — the honest-observe event, the honest-alerts notification — is source-agnostic and never branches on whether the failure came from the browser or the server (§2). exception_type is the error class or type name; message is the failure's human text; severity is its grade, one of the five SEVERITIES; environment is the deployment it happened in, one of the three ENVIRONMENTS; file, line and function locate the failure in source (line is an integer); traceback is the stack text; context is the arbitrary caller-supplied detail bag carried straight through from the raw payload's context dict; timestamp is the ISO-8601 time the boundary stamped and passed to capture, not read from a clock here (§1.5, §2). Both normalizers decompose their raw payload into exactly these fields with identical field meaning (§7.1)."

  type Fault = {
    code:    FaultCode
    message: str
  } note "a failure of a honest-errors function, returned as an ordinary value and recognized by its own shape — never raised and never wrapped in an ok/err envelope (§7.2). It is the error branch of a function's decision table, carried by the return as X | Fault with the success type still visible in the signature. The framework's chain executor (honest-type §12) recognizes a returned Fault and short-circuits the chain on it, which is why handle_failure writes no guard clause (§6). code is the closed reason the capture failed, one of the three FaultCode words; message is the human sentence naming what went wrong and, for unknown_preset, which presets are accepted (§4.1). Two fields minimum: the code to branch on and the message to read."

  # ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  # The cadence declaration (§4). The caller's data answer to "what do we do when this breaks?"
  # Each action parameter is present with a typed value or explicitly Absent — never a silent
  # default — so "the caller did not ask for a display" is a bounded case a test exercises (§4).
  # ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  type ResponseSpec = {
    mode: ResponseMode
  } note "the caller's answer to the cadence's second question: when a failure needs a response, does the caller get the normalized report back (mode 'returned') or a bare acknowledgement (mode 'acknowledged') (§4). Present as a Cadence.respond value means the failure needs a response; Absent there means it needs none and normalizing-and-recording is enough."

  type Period = {
    seconds: int
  } note "the length of a held display, in seconds, when a DisplaySpec's duration kind is 'period' (§4). seconds is the count of seconds the failure stays displayed before expire_elapsed removes it."

  type Duration = {
    kind:   DurationKind
    period: Period | Absent
  } note "how long a failure displays (§4's Duration = Once | Period, expressed as a discriminant plus its data arm). kind 'once' means a single display and carries no period (Absent); kind 'period' means a held display and carries a Period with the seconds count. The period field is present exactly when kind is 'period', so the once case has nothing to forget to fill in."

  type Scheduled = {
    at: str
  } note "the time a scheduled display is due, as an ISO-8601 string, when a DisplaySpec's timing kind is 'scheduled' (§4). `at` is when the display should go live; release_due turns the corresponding Pending entry live once now has reached it."

  type Timing = {
    kind:      TimingKind
    scheduled: Scheduled | Absent
  } note "when a failure displays (§4's Timing = Immediate | Scheduled, expressed as a discriminant plus its data arm). kind 'immediate' means it displays now and carries no scheduled time (Absent); kind 'scheduled' means it displays later and carries a Scheduled with the ISO-8601 `at` time. The scheduled field is present exactly when kind is 'scheduled'."

  type DisplaySpec = {
    duration: Duration
    timing:   Timing
  } note "the caller's answer to the cadence's last two questions: duration says whether the failure shows once or for a Period, and timing says whether it shows Immediate-ly or Scheduled for a later time (§4). Present as a Cadence.display value means the failure is displayed; Absent there means it is not displayed at all."

  type Cadence = {
    respond: ResponseSpec | Absent
    display: DisplaySpec | Absent
  } note "the caller's full handling declaration for a failure: a chain of typed action parameters, each present with a typed value or explicitly Absent (§4). respond (present or Absent) answers questions one and two — does the failure need a response, and in which mode; display (present or Absent) answers questions three and four — is it displayed, for how long and when. A reader sees every action honest-errors offers by reading this type, because the set of actions is closed and finite (§1.2). A preset is a named Cadence (§4.1); capture also accepts a Cadence directly."

  type Acknowledgement = {
    captured: bool
  } note "the bare receipt the caller gets as its response when ResponseSpec.mode is 'acknowledged': confirmation that the failure was captured, carrying no report (§4, §6). captured is true on a successful capture. build_response produces this when the cadence asks to acknowledge rather than return the report."

  # ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  # The cadence state honest-errors owns and persists (§5). Not threaded by the caller and not read
  # from any global; it lives in the module, is loaded at the start of a capture and written at the
  # end, and the orchestrator is its single mutator.
  # ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  type DedupKey = {
    exception_type: str
    file:           str
    line:           int
  } note "the display identity of a report (§5): two failures with the same exception_type, file and line are the same display. A Once display adds its key at most one live entry, so the same failure does not re-display while its entry is live. dedup_key computes one of these from an ExceptionReport."

  type LiveDisplay = {
    key:    DedupKey
    status: DisplayStatus
    until:  str | Absent
    due:    str | Absent
  } note "one entry in the cadence state: a report currently being displayed or scheduled to be (§5). key is its DedupKey identity; status is showing or pending. until is the ISO-8601 time a showing held display expires (Absent when it is not time-bounded); due is the ISO-8601 time a pending scheduled display goes live (Absent when it is not scheduled). expire_elapsed removes an entry whose until has passed; release_due flips a pending entry to showing when its due has arrived (§5)."

  type CadenceState = {
    live: list<LiveDisplay>
  } note "honest-errors' own state, the whole set of displays currently live or scheduled (§5). live is the list of LiveDisplay entries. It is loaded by load_cadence_state at the start of a capture and written by store_cadence_state at the end, persisted so a held or scheduled display survives a restart. Exactly one function mutates it: the handle_failure orchestrator, applying the next state the pure advance_cadence composition returns (§5, the single-mutator law)."

  type DisplayAction = {
    key:    DedupKey
    report: ExceptionReport
    kind:   ActionKind
  } note "one display or send the cadence makes due now — a DECISION, not a delivery (§5). key is the display identity; report is the full normalized report to display or send; kind is show, hide or send. honest-errors says what is due; honest-alerts reads the actions off the CaptureResult and performs each over its channel. honest-errors never performs the action itself (§1.4, §5)."

  type AdvanceResult = {
    state:   CadenceState
    actions: list<DisplayAction>
  } note "the pair the pure advance step returns (§5's `-> (CadenceState', list<DisplayAction>)`, named as a record): the next CadenceState and the DisplayActions due now. The pure functions return one of these and never mutate the state they were given; the orchestrator takes the state field and writes it, and returns the actions field in the CaptureResult (§5)."

  type CaptureResult = {
    report:   ExceptionReport
    response: ExceptionReport | Acknowledgement | Absent
    actions:  list<DisplayAction>
  } note "the one combined result capture returns on success (§6). report is the normalized ExceptionReport; response is what the caller gets back — the report itself when respond.mode is returned, an Acknowledgement when it is acknowledged, or Absent when respond is Absent; actions are the DisplayActions due now. The caller reads response synchronously; honest-observe records report; honest-alerts delivers each of actions. The cadence state does not appear here — honest-errors owns and persists it between calls, and the caller never threads it (§6)."

  # ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  # Step 3's dictionaries led to the vocabularies and records above; this is the dispatch data.
  # The normalizer table (§3, §6) and the preset table (§4.1), declared as dispatch so the two
  # normalizers and the four presets are named and counted, never buried in prose.
  # ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  dispatch normalize_by_source = { "js" -> classify_js_payload, "py" -> classify_py_payload } note "picks the normalizer by the source word the host passed (§3, §6): 'js' for a browser JavaScript error, 'py' for a server Python exception. The two normalizers are exactly these; a source matching neither key is the miss normalize returns an unknown_source Fault for (§6). Editing or adding a normalizer is editing this table, never control flow."

  dispatch presets = { "silent" -> preset_silent, "acknowledge" -> preset_acknowledge, "report_back" -> preset_report_back, "notify" -> preset_notify } note "resolves a preset name to its Cadence by table lookup (§4.1): the single place the four presets are enumerated, so adding or changing a preset is editing this table, never control flow. resolve_cadence dispatches a PresetName through it; a name the table does not carry is the miss resolve_cadence returns an unknown_preset Fault for."

  # ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  # Step 4's pulls and Step 1's writer: the persistence boundary onto honest-persist (§5). The
  # reader loads the current CadenceState; the writer persists the next one. The handle_failure
  # orchestrator invokes both and is the single writer. Same idiom honest-observe uses onto
  # honest-persist. These are the only I/O in the module.
  # ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  reader fn load_cadence_state : () -> CadenceState side_effect reads "honest-persist.execute" note "pulls honest-errors' own CadenceState from honest-persist through its execute query door at the start of a capture, so the orchestrator enacts the cadence against the state as it stood after the previous call (§5). It reads and does nothing else: it does not compute, mutate, or write. It takes no argument because the cadence state is the module's single owned blob, not keyed by the caller. The spec states that the state is persisted and survives a restart but does not state a fault for an unreadable store, so this reader declares none; whether a persistence read failure raises or returns is left undecided by the spec. Its one side effect is reading honest-persist."

  boundary_out fn store_cadence_state : (state: CadenceState) -> CadenceState side_effect writes "honest-persist.transaction" note "writes the next CadenceState to honest-persist through its transaction write door at the end of a capture, so a held or scheduled display survives a restart (§5). Only the handle_failure orchestrator calls it, which is what makes the orchestrator the single mutator of the cadence state (§5). state is the next CadenceState the pure advance_cadence composition returned; this writer persists it verbatim and does no computation. It returns the stored CadenceState as an acknowledgement; the orchestrator already holds the state and the actions for the result, so the return is confirmation, not data the caller needs. Its one side effect is writing honest-persist."

  # ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  # Step 5. The pure core. Normalization (§3), cadence resolution (§4.1), response building (§4,
  # §6), and the single-mutator advance composition (§5). Every function here is pure: it reads no
  # clock, no environment and no store, takes what it needs as arguments, and returns a value — a
  # Fault by return where it can fail, never a raise (§7.2, §7.3).
  # ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  # ---- normalization (§3) ----

  fn js_severity : (message: str) -> Severity note "the small pure predicate that grades a browser failure (§3): it returns 'critical' when the message text contains the token 'critical' matched case-insensitively, and 'error' for every other message. It is a predicate over the message, not an if-ladder that would grow a branch per rule. classify_js_payload calls it to fill the report's severity."

  fn classify_js_payload : (payload: JSErrorPayload | PythonExceptionPayload, environment: Environment, timestamp: str) -> ExceptionReport | Fault invokes js_severity note "normalizes a raw browser JavaScript error object into the one canonical ExceptionReport (§3). It validates the payload against the keys the JS shape requires and returns a Fault(code=malformed_payload) when a required key is absent, otherwise the report. It decomposes the browser object's message, source, lineno, colno, stack, url, user_agent, timestamp and context into the report's fields, grading severity with js_severity and stamping the environment and timestamp it was handed. environment and timestamp are arguments — the boundary that caught the failure already knew them — never read from os.getenv or a clock (§1.5, §3). The payload parameter is typed as the failure-payload union because the normalize_by_source table hands both normalizers the same value; this normalizer reads the browser shape. It returns the report on success or the malformed_payload Fault on a missing key; it never raises."

  fn classify_py_payload : (payload: JSErrorPayload | PythonExceptionPayload, environment: Environment, timestamp: str) -> ExceptionReport | Fault note "normalizes a raw server Python exception payload into the one canonical ExceptionReport (§3). It validates the payload against the keys the Python shape requires and returns a Fault(code=malformed_payload) when a required key is absent, otherwise the report. It decomposes the exception's exception_type, message, tb_file, tb_line, tb_function, traceback and context into the report's fields, stamps severity as 'error' for every Python-path report, and writes the environment and timestamp it was handed. environment and timestamp are arguments, never read from a clock or the environment (§1.5, §3). The payload parameter is typed as the failure-payload union because the normalize_by_source table hands both normalizers the same value; this normalizer reads the Python shape. It returns the report on success or the malformed_payload Fault on a missing key; it never raises."

  fn normalize : (source: str, payload: JSErrorPayload | PythonExceptionPayload, environment: Environment, timestamp: str) -> ExceptionReport | Fault invokes normalize_by_source note "the normalization step of the capture chain (§6): it dispatches the raw payload to the right normalizer by its source through the normalize_by_source table and returns the normalizer's ExceptionReport, or forwards its malformed_payload Fault. source is the raw word the host passed saying where the failure came from — 'js' or 'py'; a source the table does not carry is a miss, for which normalize returns a Fault(code=unknown_source) rather than guessing a normalizer (§6). It is source, not the payload's type, that selects the normalizer, because the host knows which boundary caught the failure. environment and timestamp are passed straight through to the chosen normalizer. It returns the report, a malformed_payload Fault from the normalizer, or an unknown_source Fault on a miss; it never raises."

  # ---- the cadence declaration and its presets (§4.1) ----

  fn preset_silent : () -> Cadence note "builds the 'silent' preset Cadence (§4.1): respond Absent, display Absent — normalize and record the failure, give no response and no display. A dispatch handler in the presets table, returning a fixed Cadence."
  fn preset_acknowledge : () -> Cadence note "builds the 'acknowledge' preset Cadence (§4.1): respond present with mode 'acknowledged', display Absent — return a bare receipt, show nothing. A dispatch handler in the presets table, returning a fixed Cadence."
  fn preset_report_back : () -> Cadence note "builds the 'report_back' preset Cadence (§4.1): respond present with mode 'returned', display Absent — return the normalized report, show nothing. A dispatch handler in the presets table, returning a fixed Cadence."
  fn preset_notify : () -> Cadence note "builds the 'notify' preset Cadence (§4.1): respond present with mode 'acknowledged', display present with a once duration and immediate timing — return a receipt and show the failure a single time, now. A dispatch handler in the presets table, returning a fixed Cadence."

  fn resolve_cadence : (arg: Cadence | PresetName) -> Cadence | Fault invokes presets note "resolves what the caller passed into a full Cadence (§4.1). A caller may hand capture a complete Cadence, which passes through unchanged, or a PresetName, which resolves to its Cadence by looking the name up in the presets dispatch table. The discrimination between a record (a Cadence) and a name (a PresetName) is a recognizer on the argument, not an if (§4.1). A name the presets table does not carry returns a Fault(code=unknown_preset) whose message names the presets it accepts. honest-errors never picks a preset from the environment or any other hidden input — the caller chooses it (§4.1). It returns the resolved Cadence or the unknown_preset Fault; it never raises."

  # ---- the response (§4, §6) ----

  fn build_response : (report: ExceptionReport, cadence: Cadence) -> ExceptionReport | Acknowledgement | Absent note "builds the caller's response from the cadence's respond spec (§6). It reads cadence.respond: when respond is Absent the response is Absent — the failure needed no response; when respond is present with mode 'returned' the response is the normalized report itself; when respond is present with mode 'acknowledged' the response is a bare Acknowledgement that the failure was captured, carrying no report. It returns one of those three and never raises; the caller reads it synchronously (§6)."

  # ---- the cadence state and its single mutator (§5) ----

  fn dedup_key : (report: ExceptionReport) -> DedupKey note "computes the display identity of a report (§5): the DedupKey of its exception_type, file and line. Two failures that agree on those three are the same display, so a Once display declared for one of them adds at most one live entry. add_display calls it to key the new report's entry."

  fn expire_elapsed : (state: CadenceState, now: str) -> AdvanceResult note "the pure step that drops displays whose held period has ended (§5). From the current CadenceState and now, it removes every LiveDisplay whose until time has passed, returning the trimmed CadenceState and the hide DisplayActions for the entries it removed. It returns a new AdvanceResult and never mutates the state it was given. now is the ISO-8601 current time, an argument, never read from a clock (§1.5, §5)."

  fn release_due : (state: CadenceState, now: str) -> AdvanceResult note "the pure step that turns scheduled displays live when their time arrives (§5). From the current CadenceState and now, it flips every Pending LiveDisplay whose due time has arrived to Showing, returning the updated CadenceState and the show (and send) DisplayActions for the entries it released. It returns a new AdvanceResult and never mutates the state it was given. now is the ISO-8601 current time, an argument (§1.5, §5)."

  fn add_display : (state: CadenceState, report: ExceptionReport, cadence: Cadence, now: str) -> AdvanceResult invokes dedup_key note "the pure step that adds the new report's display per the caller's declaration (§5). From the current CadenceState, the new report, the cadence and now, it reads cadence.display: when display is Absent it adds nothing; when present it adds a LiveDisplay keyed by dedup_key(report), Showing with an until when timing is Immediate (and a period duration sets how long it stays), or Pending with a due when timing is Scheduled. A Once display keyed to a report already live is not added again. It returns the updated CadenceState and the show DisplayAction for an immediate display (none for a scheduled one, which only becomes due later). It returns a new AdvanceResult and never mutates the state it was given. now is an argument (§1.5, §5)."

  fn advance_cadence : (state: CadenceState, report: ExceptionReport, cadence: Cadence, now: str) -> AdvanceResult invokes expire_elapsed, release_due, add_display note "the pure composition that computes the next cadence state and the actions due now (§5). From the current CadenceState, the new report, the resolved cadence and now, it expires the entries whose until has passed, releases the Pending entries whose due has arrived, and adds the new report's entry per the declaration, threading the state through the three steps and collecting their DisplayActions. It returns one AdvanceResult — the next CadenceState and the full action list — and never mutates its argument. The orchestrator, not this function, writes the returned state: this computes, the orchestrator applies, so the state has a single mutator (§5). now is an argument (§1.5, §5)."

  # ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  # Step 2's path and the orchestrator it named. The capture door hands its input to the one
  # handle_failure orchestrator, which composes resolve -> normalize -> respond -> advance as a
  # chain the honest-type §12 executor runs. The executor recognizes a link's returned Fault and
  # short-circuits, so handle_failure writes no guard clause (§6).
  # ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  orchestrator fn handle_failure : (source: str, payload: JSErrorPayload | PythonExceptionPayload, environment: Environment, timestamp: str, cadence: Cadence | PresetName, now: str) -> CaptureResult | Fault invokes resolve_cadence, normalize, build_response, load_cadence_state, advance_cadence, store_cadence_state note "the orchestrator the capture door forwards the raw failure to (§6). It composes the capture as a chain the honest-type §12 executor runs: resolve the caller's cadence (resolve_cadence), normalize the payload for its source (normalize), build the caller's response from the respond spec (build_response), load the current cadence state (load_cadence_state), advance that state and collect the actions due now (advance_cadence), and persist the next state (store_cadence_state). The executor recognizes a link's returned Fault — the error branch of its decision table — and short-circuits on it, so handle_failure writes no guard clause: a malformed_payload from a normalizer, an unknown_source from normalize, or an unknown_preset from resolve_cadence stops the chain and is returned as the Fault. It is the single mutator of the cadence state: it takes advance_cadence's returned CadenceState and writes it through store_cadence_state, and no pure function and no caller writes that state (§5). environment, timestamp and now are arguments threaded to the steps that need them, never read from the environment or a clock here (§1.5). On success it assembles one CaptureResult from the report, the response and the actions and returns it. It never raises; the host decides what to do with a returned Fault (§6)."

  boundary_in fn capture : (source: str, payload: JSErrorPayload | PythonExceptionPayload, environment: Environment, timestamp: str, cadence: Cadence | PresetName, now: str) -> CaptureResult | Fault invokes handle_failure note "the door a host error hook calls to hand honest-errors a caught failure (§6). honest-errors owns this door, not the hook that calls it: a browser's window.onerror, an HTTP framework's error handler, or @catch_at_boundary is the host's, and it catches the raw failure and calls capture (§1.5). source is the word saying where the failure came from, 'js' or 'py'; payload is the raw browser error object or Python exception; environment is the deployment it happened in, one of the three ENVIRONMENTS; timestamp is the ISO-8601 time the boundary stamped; cadence is the caller's handling declaration, a full Cadence or a PresetName; now is the ISO-8601 current time the advance step reasons against. The door does no logic: it forwards the whole call to handle_failure, which runs the cadence and returns the result. It returns one CaptureResult on success, or the Fault that stopped the chain (malformed_payload, unknown_source or unknown_preset). It never raises — honest-errors returns a fault as data and the host decides what to do with it (§6, §7.2)."

  chain capturing = capture -> handle_failure note "the capture path: the host-facing door hands its raw failure to the one orchestrator, which runs the whole cadence and returns the CaptureResult or the Fault (§6)."

  # The host error hook reaches capture as a library call. A call-form entry, so the entry-check
  # reports it UNCHECKED rather than confirming a route or decorator target.
  entry "call:capture" -> capture
